IN PLAIN WORDS
Take over the form with JavaScript: load the script with defer, listen for "submit", and call event.preventDefault() so the page doesn't reload. Read what was typed with input.value, then add it to the page: createElement → set textContent → appendChild.

CODE
```html
<h3>Applicants</h3>
<ul id="applicants-list"></ul>
<script src="join.js" defer></script>     <!-- defer: run after the HTML is loaded -->
```

```javascript
const form = document.querySelector("form");
const applicantsList = document.querySelector("#applicants-list");

form.addEventListener("submit", (event) => {
  event.preventDefault();                       // stay on the page
  const nameInput = document.querySelector("#name");
  const nameValue = nameInput.value.trim();
  if (!nameValue) return;                       // skip empty names

  const newItem = document.createElement("li"); // 1. create
  newItem.textContent = nameValue;              // 2. configure
  applicantsList.appendChild(newItem);          // 3. insert
  nameInput.value = "";
});
```

KEY POINTS
- Create → configure → insert.
- createElement makes the element in memory; appendChild puts it on the page.

IN PLAIN WORDS
Make list items interactive. Clicking an item runs classList.toggle("done"), which adds the class if it's missing and removes it if it's there. Each item gets its own Delete button, and element.remove() deletes it.

CODE
```css
.done { text-decoration: line-through; opacity: 0.5; }
```

```javascript
newItem.addEventListener("click", () => newItem.classList.toggle("done"));

const deleteButton = document.createElement("button");
deleteButton.textContent = "Delete";
deleteButton.addEventListener("click", (event) => {
  event.stopPropagation();          // don't also toggle "done" on the item
  newItem.remove();
});
newItem.appendChild(deleteButton);
```

KEY POINTS
- toggle is ideal for on/off states.
- A click on the Delete button also "bubbles up" to the <li>; stopPropagation() stops that.

IN PLAIN WORDS
The finished Join page: a form, an applicants list and a live counter. Guard clauses (if (...) return;) stop early for too many applicants (max 10), an empty name or a duplicate. childElementCount counts the list items.

STEPS: ON SUBMIT
1. preventDefault().
2. 10 or more applicants → alert and stop.
3. Read and trim the name; empty → stop.
4. Same name already listed (ignoring case) → alert and stop.
5. Create the <li> with a name, a Delete button and click-to-toggle done.
6. Append it, update the counter, clear the input.

CODE
```javascript
const form = document.querySelector("form");
const applicantsList = document.querySelector("#applicants-list");
const applicantsCount = document.querySelector("#applicants-count");

function updateCount() {
  applicantsCount.textContent = "Total applicants: " + applicantsList.childElementCount;
}

form.addEventListener("submit", (event) => {
  event.preventDefault();
  if (applicantsList.childElementCount >= 10) {
    alert("Maximum of 10 applicants reached. Please remove someone before adding more.");
    return;
  }
  const nameInput = document.querySelector("#name");
  const nameValue = nameInput.value.trim();
  if (!nameValue) return;
  for (const item of applicantsList.querySelectorAll("li")) {
    if (item.dataset.name.toLowerCase() === nameValue.toLowerCase()) {
      alert("This player already has an application in the list.");
      return;
    }
  }

  const newItem = document.createElement("li");
  newItem.dataset.name = nameValue;              // the name alone, without "Delete"
  newItem.textContent = nameValue;
  newItem.addEventListener("click", () => newItem.classList.toggle("done"));

  const deleteButton = document.createElement("button");
  deleteButton.textContent = "Delete";
  deleteButton.addEventListener("click", (e) => {
    e.stopPropagation();
    newItem.remove();
    updateCount();
  });
  newItem.appendChild(deleteButton);

  applicantsList.appendChild(newItem);
  updateCount();
  nameInput.value = "";
});
```

```css
#applicants-list { padding-left: 0; margin-top: 8px; }
#applicants-list li {
  list-style: none;
  margin: 6px 0;
  padding: 6px 10px;
  border: 1px solid #4fd1ff;
  border-radius: 4px;
  background-color: #181818;
  display: flex;
  justify-content: space-between;
  align-items: center;
}
```

KEY POINTS
- Guard clauses keep the main path flat and readable.
- data-* attributes (dataset) store values on an element for your code.

CORRECTION
The lesson's duplicate check used item.textContent.startsWith(nameValue). Each item's text includes its button ("AlexDelete"), so adding "Al" after "Alex" was wrongly blocked, "alex" and "Alex" counted as different people, and a name of only spaces got through (checked by running it). The code above compares the stored name exactly, ignoring case, and trims spaces.

IN PLAIN WORDS
async lets a function pause; await pauses only that function until a Promise finishes, and the rest of the page keeps running. fetch(url) gives a Response (the envelope: status and headers); response.json() reads and parses the body (the letter inside). That's two awaits. Use Live Server (http://), not a double-clicked file (file://), for API work.

CODE
```javascript
async function loadData() {
  console.log("A");
  const result = await getSomething();
  console.log("B", result);
}
console.log("C");
loadData();
console.log("D");
// prints: C, A, D, B
```

```javascript
async function main() {
  const response = await fetch("https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1");   // the envelope
  const data = await response.json();                                             // the contents
  console.log(data.id, data.title);
}
main();
```

KEY POINTS
- An async function always returns a Promise.
- await fetch → the HTTP response. await response.json() → JavaScript data.
- Changing the URL (/posts/3) changes which record comes back, not its shape.
- Pages opened as file:// can be blocked from calling APIs; open them with Live Server.

IN PLAIN WORDS
for (const item of items) loops through an array; use of, not in. Each item is one object, so pick fields with item.title. To show them: createElement("li") → textContent → appendChild. The full chain: fetch → response.json() → for...of → createElement → textContent → appendChild.

CODE
```html
<ul id="list"></ul>
<script src="main.js" defer></script>
```

```javascript
async function loadList() {
  const response = await fetch("https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos?_limit=5");
  const items = await response.json();
  const ul = document.getElementById("list");
  for (const item of items) {
    const li = document.createElement("li");
    li.textContent = item.title;
    ul.appendChild(li);
  }
}
loadList();
```

KEY POINTS
- APIs usually return arrays of objects; for...of gives you one object at a time.
- for...in loops over keys (indexes), which is why for...of is the one for arrays.

IN PLAIN WORDS
A book search page using the Open Library API: read the input with .value.trim(), show a message if it's empty, show "Loading...", build the URL with encodeURIComponent, fetch and parse, clear the list, and show the first 10 results (title — author) or "No results found".

STEPS
1. On click, read and trim the search text; empty → show "Please type something to search."
2. Show "Loading..." in the list.
3. Build the URL with encodeURIComponent(searchTerm).
4. fetch, check response.ok, then response.json().
5. Clear the list, take data.docs.slice(0, 10).
6. None → "No results found". Otherwise one <li> per book.
7. Any network failure → show an error message instead of leaving "Loading..." forever.

CODE
```javascript
const searchInput = document.getElementById("search-input");
const resultsList = document.getElementById("results-list");

function showMessage(text) {
  resultsList.innerHTML = "";
  const li = document.createElement("li");
  li.textContent = text;
  resultsList.appendChild(li);
}

document.getElementById("search-button").addEventListener("click", () => {
  const searchTerm = searchInput.value.trim();
  if (!searchTerm) return showMessage("Please type something to search.");
  searchBooks(searchTerm);
});

async function searchBooks(searchTerm) {
  showMessage("Loading...");
  const url = `https://openlibrary.org/search.json?q=${encodeURIComponent(searchTerm)}`;
  try {
    const response = await fetch(url);
    if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);
    const data = await response.json();
    const books = data.docs.slice(0, 10);
    if (books.length === 0) return showMessage(`No results found for "${searchTerm}".`);
    resultsList.innerHTML = "";
    for (const book of books) {
      const li = document.createElement("li");
      const author = book.author_name?.[0] ?? "Unknown author";
      li.textContent = `${book.title || "No title"} — ${author}`;
      resultsList.appendChild(li);
    }
  } catch (error) {
    showMessage("Search failed. Please try again.");
  }
}
```

KEY POINTS
- encodeURIComponent makes spaces and symbols safe inside a URL.
- innerHTML = "" clears old results before new ones.
- An empty string is falsy, so if (!searchTerm) catches it.

CORRECTION
The lesson's version had no error handling: if the network failed or the API was down, "Loading..." stayed forever. The code above checks response.ok and catches errors.

IN PLAIN WORDS
An info card is a small reusable layout: a box with a title and labeled rows (<span class="label"> + <span class="value">). It works for countries, kingdoms, NPCs, items or quests. Let it grow downward (no fixed height) and center it with flexbox on the body.

CODE
```html
<section class="info-card">
  <h2 class="info-title">Kingdom of Eldrich</h2>
  <p class="info-row"><span class="label">Capital:</span> <span class="value">Moonspire</span></p>
  <p class="info-row"><span class="label">Ruler:</span> <span class="value">Queen Aria</span></p>
</section>
```

```css
body {
  min-height: 100vh;
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;   /* horizontally */
  align-items: center;       /* vertically */
  margin: 0;
  font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;
}
.info-card {
  border: 1px solid #ccc;
  padding: 16px;
  max-width: 320px;
  border-radius: 8px;
  box-shadow: 0 4px 10px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
}
.info-row { margin: 4px 0; }
.info-row .label { font-weight: 600; margin-right: 4px; }
.info-row .value { opacity: 0.9; }
```

KEY POINTS
- span wraps part of a line so the label and value can be styled separately.
- Never give a card a fixed height; let rows add downward.

IN PLAIN WORDS
Let users change the card without reloading. Each field gets an input, a button, and a class on the text it changes. On click, read input.value and set the element's textContent. One helper function can wire up every field.

CODE
```html
<span class="capital-value">Moonspire</span>
...
<input id="capital-input" type="text" placeholder="New capital" />
<button id="capital-button" type="button">Update Capital</button>
```

```javascript
function wireField(inputId, buttonId, fieldSelector) {
  const input = document.querySelector(inputId);
  const field = document.querySelector(fieldSelector);
  document.querySelector(buttonId).addEventListener("click", () => {
    const value = input.value.trim();
    if (value) field.textContent = value;
  });
}

wireField("#kingdom-input", "#kingdom-button", ".kingdom-name");
wireField("#threat-input", "#threat-button", ".threat-value");
wireField("#region-input", "#region-button", ".region-value");
wireField("#ruler-input", "#ruler-button", ".ruler-value");
wireField("#capital-input", "#capital-button", ".capital-value");
```

```css
.kingdom-item {
